WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.871
This array contains three firms in each of
four markets.

2
00:00:03.871 --> 00:00:09.152
In this small example, divide every weight
by its own column sum to obtain shares.

3
00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:15.112
A Python loop selects one market, calls

4
00:00:15.112 --> 00:00:18.268
array operations, and then visits the next
market.

5
00:00:18.268 --> 00:00:20.635
The same pattern repeats four times.

6
00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:27.484
Vectorization expresses the update for
every column together.

7
00:00:27.484 --> 00:00:30.226
The array library performs the bulk work

8
00:00:30.226 --> 00:00:34.144
in compiled code, with fewer trips through
the Python loop.

9
00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:39.691
Both routes make twelve divisions and four
column sums.

10
00:00:39.691 --> 00:00:41.547
They produce the same shares.

11
00:00:41.547 --> 00:00:43.403
Vectorization removes the loop's overhead,

12
00:00:43.403 --> 00:00:47.486
so the speedup is smaller when each market
has more firms.

13
00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:53.299
An array expression does not promise
multiple CPU cores or a GPU.

14
00:00:53.299 --> 00:00:56.186
Each market still has its own denominator,

15
00:00:56.186 --> 00:00:59.486
and an iterative solver still needs
convergence checks.
